    <item>
      <title>A plethora of asteroids in TESS data</title>
      <link>http://localhost:1313/research/2019-03-17-a-plethora-of-asteroids-in-tess-data/</link>
      <pubDate>Sun, 17 Mar 2019 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate>
      <guid>http://localhost:1313/research/2019-03-17-a-plethora-of-asteroids-in-tess-data/</guid>
      <description>&lt;p&gt;TESS is the &lt;a href=&#34;https://tess.mit.edu/&#34;&gt;Transiting Exoplanet Survey Satellite&lt;/a&gt;, which was launched into space by NASA in 2018 to identify faint brightness variations in stars that are characteristic of planets orbiting around and transiting in front of these stars.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;div class=&#34;wrapper&#34;
     style=&#34;display: block; margin-left: auto; margin-right: auto; 
            margin-bottom: 20px; width: 90%;&#34;&gt;
  &lt;figure class=&#34;image&#34;
	  style=&#34;display: block; margin-left: auto; margin-right: auto; 
                 width: ;&#34;&gt;
    &lt;a href=&#34;https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/c/c2/Transiting_Exoplanet_Survey_Satellite_artist_concept_%28transparent_background%29.png&#34;&gt;
        &lt;img src=&#34;https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/c/c2/Transiting_Exoplanet_Survey_Satellite_artist_concept_%28transparent_background%29.png&#34; alt=&#34;&#34;  align=&#34;center&#34;&gt;
    &lt;/a&gt;
    &lt;figcaption style=&#34;font-style: italic; text-align: justify;&#34;&gt;
      A rendering of the TESS spacecraft. The instrument consists of four imaging cameras, each covering 24° x 24° of the sky.
    &lt;/figcaption&gt;
  &lt;/figure&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Over the course of its nominal two-year mission, TESS will monitor more than 200,000 stars with the photometric accuracy necessary to identify &lt;a href=&#34;https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Exoplanet&#34;&gt;exoplanet&lt;/a&gt; transits. In order to observe this huge number of stars, TESS points at different &amp;ldquo;Sectors&amp;rdquo; in the sky, amounting to a total of 85% of the entire sky. Each Sector spanning 24° x 96° is observed over a period of about one month.&lt;/p&gt;</description>
